WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.583
A voiceover script is text that nobody reads.

2
00:00:03.633 --> 00:00:06.636
They only hear it, once, at the speed it's spoken.

3
00:00:06.686 --> 00:00:08.449
That changes the rules.

4
00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:10.967
Short sentences add weight.

5
00:00:11.017 --> 00:00:13.207
Longer ones carry the listener along.

6
00:00:13.257 --> 00:00:17.702
And give each sentence just one idea, so nobody has to hold two at once.

7
00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:21.226
Full stops and questions create pauses.

8
00:00:21.276 --> 00:00:24.058
Use them to set up what you want remembered.

9
00:00:24.108 --> 00:00:25.881
So how many finished the study?

10
00:00:25.931 --> 00:00:27.218
Just over half.

11
00:00:28.468 --> 00:00:32.505
Spoken jargon is harder to decode, because there's no time to stop.

12
00:00:32.555 --> 00:00:34.432
Say use, not utilise.

13
00:00:34.482 --> 00:00:36.197
Help, not facilitate.

14
00:00:36.247 --> 00:00:38.346
Define technical terms once.

15
00:00:38.846 --> 00:00:42.755
Preview what's coming, mark each transition, then recap.

16
00:00:42.805 --> 00:00:45.135
It feels repetitive on paper.

17
00:00:45.185 --> 00:00:47.598
In audio, it's what keeps people with you.

18
00:00:48.246 --> 00:00:50.193
Put your script in the speaker notes.

19
00:00:50.243 --> 00:00:54.744
On Pro, AI directs the video and times the animation to your voice.

20
00:00:54.794 --> 00:00:57.242
Try it at powerpoint2vid.com.

